#!/usr/bin/env -S uv run --script
# /// script
# requires-python = ">=3.11"
# dependencies = [
#     "capabilities-contract>=0.33,<1",
#     "httpx>=0.27",
#     "puremagic>=1.28",
#     "qrcode>=7.4",
#     "neonize @ https://github.com/ai-cluster-one/neonize/releases/download/v0.5.2-histsync.1/neonize-0.5.2-py3-none-any.whl",
# ]
# ///
"""
whatsapp — WhatsApp CLI carrying its own engine: one linked device, one store.

Agent-invoked. Links a WhatsApp account as a companion device, keeps the account's
chats and messages in the project's Postgres database, reads from it, deepens it
on demand, downloads and transcribes voice notes, and renders an export a project
can commit to. Run `whatsapp doctor` first to confirm the chain.

Every verb connects, works, and exits: the engine is whatsmeow, compiled into
the installed wheel and driven in-process for the life of one invocation. The
bundled assistant service is the one exception: it holds the connection and
captures live for as long as it runs (see Assistant service).

Startup protocol
  1. `whatsapp doctor` — proves the engine loads, the session exists and
     authorises, and the account matches the connection's binding. It also drains
     whatever the server held for the device while nothing was connected.
  2. Exit 7 means the device is no longer linked: re-link with `whatsapp pair`.
  3. Exit 8 means another invocation, or the running assistant service, holds
     this session; wait and retry, or stop the service. Two clients on one
     linked device desync it, so the lock is never bypassed.
  4. Exit 2/4/6 are credentials, policy, and input as everywhere else.

Connections
  Configuration requires explicit named connections. A connection is one WhatsApp
  identity. A project with several declares them as its own connections, or one
  machine-level registry serves every project at
  ~/.config/whatsapp/connections.json; where this project reads them from,
  "Where connections live" below says:
        {
          "default": "<id>",
          "connections": {
            "<id>": {
              "engine":              "whatsmeow",
              "number":              "15551234567",
              "expected_account_id": "15551234567",
              "device_name":         "Capability (<host>)",
              "initial_sync_days":   30,
              "messages_dir":        "messages",
              "allow_write":         false
            }
          }
        }

  engine               "whatsmeow" (the default — this CLI's own engine, no
                       server) or "waha" (a self-hosted WAHA bridge).
  number               the account's phone number, digits with country code. The
                       default for `pair`, and what names the account's state
                       home, so an in-house entry declares it (or pins
                       expected_account_id) before it can pair.
  expected_account_id  the account this session must be, and the identity that
                       names its state home when it is set. `pair` reports the
                       value to bind; a session belonging to a different account
                       is then refused rather than silently used.
  device_name          what the entry is called under Linked Devices on the
                       phone. Default: "Capability (<this machine>)".
  initial_sync_days    how far back the one-time pairing burst reaches
                       (default 30). Depth is separable from reach: a shallow
                       pairing does not cap what `messages` can later fetch.
  messages_dir         where `export` writes. Absolute, or relative to the
                       project's capabilities/whatsapp/ folder.

  A "waha" connection instead carries base_url, session, secret_env,
  dashboard_user_env / dashboard_pass_env, waha_engine ("GOWS") and tier. An
  entry carrying base_url is a bridge entry whether or not it names its engine,
  since only a bridge has an address to reach; `whatsapp connections` reports
  the resolved engine and where that answer came from. A flag one engine does
  not take is refused rather than ignored.

  WRITES ARE OFF BY DEFAULT: a WhatsApp message reaches a real person, so `send`
  exits 4 unless the connection carries an explicit "allow_write": true. No flag
  lifts the gate; granting it is a deliberate registry edit, made after asking
  the user.

  Selection: --connection <id> (-c) on every command. Unflagged, the registry's
  "default" pointer applies, else the sole entry; several entries without a
  pointer is refused as ambiguous.

  Outside any project, connections and doctor are machine reads: they use the
  machine's own connections read-only, unless a grant switched one off. pair
  uses them there too, so a connection declared globally is linked on the
  machine; every other verb that needs a connection is refused there.

Credentials (4-tier cascade; first non-empty wins)
  flags > project .env(.local) > ~/.config/whatsapp/credentials.env > process env.
        DEEPGRAM_API_KEY   only `whatsapp transcribe` reads it. Shared: kept once
                           for the machine in ~/.config/capabilities/shared.env,
                           which `whatsapp setup set` writes, asked before
                           whatsapp's own credentials.env.
  The linked-device session is not a configured secret: it is minted by pairing
  and lives in the user state home, per account.

Store
  Chats, messages, identities, enrichment and capture provenance live in the
  project's Postgres database, in whatsapp_* tables keyed by the account,
  created and migrated on first use. The database is the first that answers
  for the project: AGENTKIT_DB_* in its .env.local / .env, then the process
  environment, then the machine's store setting (`capabilities store set`);
  `capabilities store show` shows which answers. With none, every verb that
  reads or writes the capture exits 6 store_not_configured; help, contract,
  connections and the other contract commands still answer. History an
  earlier version kept in a messages.db file in the state home is not read by
  any verb until `whatsapp import-legacy` carries it into the store, once.

State
  $XDG_STATE_HOME/whatsapp/<account>/     named for the account, not for the
                                          connection: a connection can be
                                          renamed without losing the session
                                          or the capture
        session.db     the engine's own — device, keys, contacts, LID map
        linked-by.json the connection that linked the device, and the project
                       whose own connection it is (null: the machine's shared
                       one); `connection add` and `pair` refuse another
                       connection the same number (exit 6 number_linked,
                       naming it under `holder`), and another project is given
                       that connection by a grant
        raw/           history chunks exactly as received, written before parsing
        media/         downloaded attachments, keyed by message id
        lock           held for the duration of any connected verb, and by
                       the assistant service for its whole life
        service/       the assistant service's control files on this
                       machine: owner.json, health.json, daemon.pid,
                       daemon.log, and capture-spool.jsonl while the store
                       is unreachable (capture-spool.rejected.jsonl keeps
                       a spooled capture the store refused for its content)

Assistant service
  `whatsapp service ...` runs a bundled long-running listener, `assistant`, on
  one account. It holds the account's connection for as long as it runs,
  through the same engine, capture path, account check and store every verb
  uses, so each message is written to whatsapp_messages as it arrives (sync
  type LIVE). A dropped connection is re-established with backoff (2s
  doubling to 60s), and the offline queue drains on each reconnect. A device
  removed from the phone is final: the service stops reconnecting and says
  so in `service status` and `service doctor`. It sends the messages `send`
  queues for its account, oldest first and at most `defaults.send_rate` a
  minute; a queued row wakes the sender (in-process at once, or within about
  a second from any other process, through the account's whatsapp_outbox
  version in the store's change versions, which the listener polls on its
  own store connection), with a half-second poll behind it. A capture the store cannot take because the store is unreachable is
  appended to a spool file in the service's state directory and written into
  the store, in order and once, when it answers again (and at start);
  `service status` reports the spool depth and the store error. A send a
  previous run left in flight is failed as interrupted_delivery_unknown at
  start and never resent.
  Liveness: a heartbeat thread publishes health every 15s whatever the
  engine and the dialogue are doing, and a watchdog thread checks that the
  listener's loop keeps going round and that no engine handler runs on; when
  either has made no progress for `defaults.stall_seconds` (default 120) the
  listener writes every thread's stack to its log and leaves with exit 9 for
  its supervisor to restart. SIGTERM is honoured within 30s even when the
  loop is blocked (exit 9 when it had to be forced). A `service run` or
  `service start` that finds the account held by a listener whose health is
  older than `stall_seconds` ends it (SIGTERM, then SIGKILL after 15s) and
  takes over; a holder with fresh health is respected (exit 8). Health older
  than 60s from a live holder is `stale`: `service status` reports it with
  its age, and `service doctor` - the deploy doctor - fails on it.
  Dialogue: it answers admitted messages with a dialogue turn. Each live
  capture passes a gate in this order: its own message (never answered,
  except what the owner types from the phone in the account's own self-chat),
  history-sync origin (never), older than `max_age` (dropped), already in the
  register (dropped), chat allowed, sender allowed (people are named by phone
  number and matched through the store's phone/LID identities), addressed (a
  group needs our JID or LID mentioned, a quote of our message, or an alias in
  the text unless `require_reference` is false; a trailing `#noreply` line
  opts out everywhere), role, then a control command (/status, /set, /reload,
  /stop, /help, gated by control roles) or a turn. An admitted message is
  reserved in whatsapp_register before anything else, so a redelivery or a
  restart never answers it twice, and nothing before the service's first
  sight of a chat is answered. A broadcast (status@broadcast, a broadcast
  list, a channel post) is captured and never judged. The moment a message is
  admitted for a turn it is marked read and the chat shows the account
  composing, refreshed until the turn ends and paused when it ends without
  an answer. Turns run per chat in arrival order after
  `debounce`, at most `max_parallel_dialogue` at once, through
  callva-harness-runner on a profile kept in the store, as agentrooms and
  tasks keep theirs (the project's version, then the global one, then the
  runner's shipped profiles, then the bundled whatsapp-claude, the default,
  and whatsapp-codex; see `whatsapp profiles`), fit-checked at start, reload
  and in `service doctor`, ended at `worker_timeout`. The
  prompt is the context document, the chat's `context`, the channel state,
  the request and the last `tail_size` stored messages; the role's
  `authority` goes to the run as CAPABILITIES_AUTH_CONTEXT. The answer, cut
  at `=== REPLY ===`, is queued as pending rows (quoting the request in a
  group, split between paragraphs) and sent by the listener with no pause of
  its own, as is a job's result; an empty answer is silent. The listener resolves the runner itself (`uv run --with`); the
  CLI does not carry it.
  Profiles are saved in the store with `tasks profiles save` (the table is
  callva-harness-runner's, shared by the family) and taken at start and
  `service reload`. A project's profile files in
  capabilities/whatsapp/service/profiles/ are no longer read: while any is
  there, start and doctor refuse, exit 6 `profiles_not_imported`, until
  `whatsapp profiles import --apply` moves them into the store.
  Voice notes: `voice_transcription` (`/set voice-transcription`, else the
  group's or person's, else `defaults`, else `addressed`) decides which are
  transcribed before the turn: `off` none; `addressed` every admitted one in
  a direct chat and, in a group, one that quotes our message; `auto` every
  one in a group, answered only when its transcript matches an alias. The
  note is reserved first, fetched through the listener's own connection and
  sent to Deepgram (DEEPGRAM_API_KEY through the credential cascade); the
  transcript is stored in whatsapp_enrichment as `transcribe` stores it, and
  each transcribed note is echoed into the chat before any turn (a direct
  chat: `Твоё сообщение:` and the quoted transcript; a group: the quoted
  transcript replying to the note; a failure: `[голосовое — не удалось
  расшифровать]`). A turn, when one runs, reads `[voice] <transcript>`, or
  `[voice note - transcription failed: <reason>]`, never retried. The tail
  shows every stored transcript under its sender and leaves the echoes out.
  Jobs: a turn whose role's authority reaches `whatsapp jobs` is told how to
  hand longer work to a job (the `delegation` document) and runs with the
  bundle's worker shim first on PATH, which fixes the chat, the requester, the
  origin message and the job profile from the turn's environment and refuses
  a call that names any of them. Submitting a draft ends the turn: its own
  short line, or after a grace period the job's description, is the
  acknowledgement. The listener's job runner claims waiting jobs in arrival
  order within `defaults.max_parallel_jobs`, each with a slot row, a lease
  and a fencing token in whatsapp_jobs / whatsapp_job_slots, and runs each
  through callva-harness-runner on its profile (`defaults.job_profile`,
  bundled whatsapp-job-claude) with the `job-worker` document as its prompt,
  recording pid and session at start. An amendment stops the attempt and
  continues the same session. The result is queued once, as pending rows
  quoting the message that asked for it. A spent quota stops the job as
  `quota` and pauses the queue until it lifts; a refused model or any other
  failure is recorded and reported to the chat. A job whose listener went
  away is interrupted and, per `defaults.job_recovery`, continued on its
  session (`requeue`) or stopped and reported (`inspect`); a result not yet
  delivered is delivered after a restart, once. /status lists the chat's
  open jobs and /stop stops them with the running turn.
  Its settings are the project's whatsapp service settings records
  (capabilities/whatsapp/service/settings.json in files mode): `connection`,
  `environment`, `assistant_name`, `direct_messages {mode allowlist|anyone|
  off, default_role}`, `allowed_users {<phone>: {name, role, profile,
  worker_timeout, context, voice_transcription}}`, `allowed_groups
  {<jid>@g.us: {name, require_reference, aliases, may_address
  anyone|allowed_users|[phones], member_role, profile, worker_timeout,
  context, voice_transcription}}`, `control {roles}`, `authority {default,
  roles}` and `defaults {tail_size, debounce, max_age, worker_timeout,
  max_parallel_dialogue, profile, send_rate, max_parallel_jobs, job_profile,
  job_recovery requeue|inspect, voice_transcription off|addressed|auto,
  stall_seconds 30..3600}`. A top-level
  `send_rate` is read as `defaults.send_rate`. Any other key is refused.
  Settings that admit nobody leave the listener capturing and sending and
  answering nothing.
  Activation needs an explicit project enable (`capabilities enable whatsapp
  --project`) and a configured store. While the service runs, `send` on that
  account hands its message to the service and returns the service's answer,
  every other verb that connects on that account exits 8 naming the service,
  and reads that answer from the store (`status`, `health`, `chats` without
  --fresh, `messages` the store covers or with --rounds 0) keep working.
      whatsapp service init [--connection ID] [--force]
                                       Seed the settings records.
      whatsapp service start [--connection ID]
                                       Start the listener detached; returns once
                                       it holds the account (taking it over
                                       from a stale holder).
      whatsapp service run [--connection ID]
                                       Run the listener in the foreground (the
                                       deploy command). Exit 9 when it stalled.
      whatsapp service stop [--connection ID] [--timeout S] [--force]
      whatsapp service reload [--connection ID] [--timeout S]
                                       Re-read and validate the settings in the
                                       running listener, without reconnecting.
      whatsapp service status [--connection ID]
                                       state, pid, connected, uptime, last event.
      whatsapp service logs [--connection ID] [--tail N]
      whatsapp service doctor [--connection ID]
                                       Settings, store, link and ownership.
      whatsapp service harness [--json]
                                       The harness tools (claude, codex) of
                                       every profile a turn is configured to
                                       run on now - each the settings reach,
                                       every chat's `/set profile` and each
                                       open job's - resolved and fitted as a
                                       turn resolves it, for the sign-ins a
                                       node needs. With no dialogue admitted
                                       none. Runs no turn and writes nothing;
                                       `unresolved` names a profile that does
                                       not resolve or fit.
      whatsapp jobs help               The job register's verbs: list, active,
                                       show, register, submit, amend, discard,
                                       stop, resume.

Commands
  whatsapp help                        Print this usage block
  whatsapp doctor [--connection ID]    Readiness — the engine loads, a session
                                       exists, it authorises, and the account
                                       matches the binding. Connects, so it also
                                       drains the server's queue for the device.
  whatsapp status                      Human-readable account / session / store view
  whatsapp health                      Store readiness — what the capture holds
                                       per chat, its span, and what is missing.
                                       Reads the store only; answers "is there
                                       anything to consume?" without connecting
                                       to WhatsApp.
  whatsapp pair [--number DIGITS] [--qr] [--days N] [--full] [--hold SECONDS]
                [--wait] [--recreate --yes] [--events]
                                       Link this machine as a companion device.
                                       Prints an XXXX-XXXX code to enter on the
                                       phone (Settings → Linked Devices → Link a
                                       Device → "Link with phone number"); --qr
                                       paints a scannable QR code on the progress
                                       stream instead, repainting it whenever the
                                       reference expires. The symbol is wide — it
                                       says so when the terminal is too narrow to
                                       hold it.
                                       Either way the invocation holds the socket
                                       the code was minted on until the phone
                                       completes the link — a link cannot complete
                                       against a client that has exited — and then
                                       holds it further to capture the one-time
                                       history burst, which arrives once and is
                                       not repeated on reconnect.
                                       --events drives it with no terminal: JSON
                                       lines on stdout instead of the painted
                                       symbol (qr, each new reference as it
                                       arrives; pairing_code; phone_needed, when
                                       no number is known, answered by one line
                                       on stdin; linked; progress; done; error).
                                       A connection that names no account yet is
                                       linked all the same, and the account the
                                       link answers for is written to it as
                                       expected_account_id (`identity_bound` in
                                       the result). A first link under --events
                                       that fails or is cancelled (SIGTERM,
                                       SIGINT, SIGHUP) removes the connection
                                       and the state it made.
                                       --days sets the burst window (default: the
                                       connection's initial_sync_days, else 30);
                                       --full asks for everything the phone still
                                       holds; --hold caps how long the burst is
                                       waited for (default 180s) — it arrives once,
                                       so a large account wants a larger value.
                                       --recreate --yes re-links a session that
                                       already exists. --wait is the WAHA form.
  whatsapp chats [--search TERM] [--limit N] [--fresh] [--json]
                                       List chats from the store, most recent
                                       first. --fresh connects first, so the
                                       server's queue is drained before answering.
  whatsapp messages <chat_id> [--limit N] [--from YYYY-MM-DD] [--to YYYY-MM-DD]
                              [--rounds N] [--fresh] [--format conversation|table]
                              [--json]
                                       Read a chat. Answers from the store, and
                                       reaches back to the phone when the store
                                       cannot cover the request — depth is a
                                       parameter of reading, not a separate verb.
                                       Each round asks the phone for 50 older
                                       messages and takes about 4 seconds;
                                       --rounds caps them (default 10, 0 disables
                                       reaching). The phone must be online to
                                       answer; its absence is reported as itself.
  whatsapp contact <contact_id> [--fresh] [--json]
                                       Show a contact's name, number, and both
                                       identity forms (phone JID and LID).
  whatsapp export <chat_id> [--out PATH] [--media-dir PATH] [--limit N]
                            [--from YYYY-MM-DD] [--to YYYY-MM-DD] [--rounds N]
                            [--page-size N] [--max-pages N] [--no-photos]
                            [--no-stickers] [--videos] [--documents]
                                       Render a chat from the store into
                                       messages.json + media/. Idempotent —
                                       re-runs preserve transcriptions and skip
                                       already-downloaded media. Without --out,
                                       writes to the connection's messages_dir
                                       (<messages_dir>/<slug>/messages.json).
                                       Media leaves WhatsApp's CDN while the keys
                                       stay valid, so what can no longer be fetched
                                       is recorded as lost rather than omitted or
                                       retried forever. --page-size / --max-pages
                                       page a WAHA connection instead.
  whatsapp transcribe <json_path> [--force] [--model nova-3]
                                       Fill transcription placeholders for
                                       voice/audio messages in an exported JSON
                                       using Deepgram (requires DEEPGRAM_API_KEY).
                                       Results are also kept in the store, so a
                                       later export carries them without redoing
                                       the work.
  whatsapp render <json_path> [--out PATH]
                                       Pretty-print an exported messages.json as
                                       a markdown conversation log (date headers,
                                       sender names, voice transcriptions inlined).
                                       stdout by default; --out writes a file.
  whatsapp import-legacy [--file PATH]  Import, once, the history an earlier
                                       version kept in messages.db in the
                                       account's state home: chats, messages,
                                       identities, enrichment, chunk records and
                                       meta. Reads the file read-only and leaves
                                       it as it is. Rows the store already holds
                                       win; identities and enrichment fill what
                                       the store lacks. A second run imports
                                       nothing. Exit 3 when there is no file, 6
                                       when it is not a legacy store.
  whatsapp profiles list [NAME ...]    Each profile the service settings reach
                                       (or each NAME) as it resolves from this
                                       project: its layer (project, global,
                                       shipped, capability), stored version,
                                       harness, model, and whether it fits.
  whatsapp profiles import [--apply]   Move this project's profile files from
                                       capabilities/whatsapp/service/profiles/
                                       into the store at its scope, all or
                                       none; --apply saves them and removes the
                                       files. Exit 6 naming a file that cannot
                                       be read or differs from the version the
                                       project already holds.
  whatsapp send <chat_id> <text> [--reply-to MESSAGE_ID]
                [--mention PHONE]... [--typing]
                                       Send one text message. Write-enabled
                                       connections only: `send` exits 4 unless
                                       the connection carries allow_write,
                                       before anything is written.
                                       The message is first a pending row in
                                       the store. With the assistant service
                                       running on the account the service sends
                                       it and `send` waits up to 60s for its
                                       answer; otherwise `send` connects and
                                       sends it. Reports the id the account
                                       minted, which is what a later read
                                       carries, with local_id (the row's
                                       internal id), delivery and sent_by
                                       (service or direct). A failed send exits
                                       5 send_failed; no answer in time exits 5
                                       send_pending naming the local id, and
                                       the row is still sent when the service
                                       can, so sending again sends twice.
                                       --reply-to quotes a message of the same
                                       chat the store holds (exit 3 otherwise);
                                       --mention mentions a number, repeatable,
                                       and its @number leads the text unless
                                       the text carries it; --typing shows
                                       composing for 1.5-3.5s, then paused,
                                       before sending.
                                       What goes out is an ordinary message,
                                       carrying no machine marking of any kind,
                                       so saying who is writing is the sender's
                                       job and belongs in the text.

Chat and contact IDs
  DMs    — <number>@s.whatsapp.net  e.g. 15551234567@s.whatsapp.net
  Groups — <group-id>@g.us
  LIDs   — <opaque>@lid, the privacy-preserving form group senders arrive as.
  A bare number, and the <number>@c.us form, are accepted and normalised.

I/O contract
  Success → stdout: JSON for structured results (doctor, health, --json, the
  export / transcribe summaries) or a human-readable view for the read and render
  commands. One message envelope is emitted everywhere a message appears — by
  `messages --json` and in an export's "messages" array — and
  `whatsapp contract` prints it, built by the same code that produces it.
  Failure → a one-line JSON envelope on stderr:
        {"error": {"code", "message", "hint"?, "status"?}}
  Progress chatter from pair / export / transcribe goes to stderr; stdout stays
  clean. Engine and protocol logs are stderr too.

Contract commands (the same on every capability):
  whatsapp contract                            The message envelope every read
                                               emits, as a worked example.
  whatsapp connections                         Where every connection's values
                                               resolve from (tier, source, secrets
                                               masked). Local only — no network.
                                               Each connection carries `session`
                                               {live, account}: `live` is true when
                                               the engine's session on this machine
                                               holds a linked device and the
                                               service here has not recorded it
                                               logged out; `account` is the account
                                               the device is linked as, else the
                                               connection's recorded identity, else
                                               its expected_account_id, or null. A
                                               waha connection's session is held by
                                               the WAHA server, so `live` is null
                                               and `reason` is `waha_server`.
  whatsapp stub                                One-line awareness text.
  whatsapp manifest --json                     Machine-readable declaration;
                                               `connections.device_bound` is true:
                                               a connection is a linked device.
  whatsapp guide [<topic>]                     Guide topics / one guide body,
                                               fetched live from the docs base.
  whatsapp ids list|get <l>|set <l> <v>|rm <l> The project identifiers envelope
           [--note <text>]                     (capabilities/whatsapp/identifiers.json,
                                                or store rows once the agent moved).
  whatsapp refs                                Menu of the project's reference
                                               files for this capability.
  whatsapp inventory [--network]               What this capability holds in
                                               this project (--network adds
                                               remote detail).
  whatsapp migrate [status]                    Apply the store's pending steps
                                               to the whatsapp_* tables;
                                               `status` reports where they
                                               stand, without a lock, changing
                                               nothing.

Exit codes
  0  success
  2  auth — missing / rejected credentials, or the session authorises as an
     account other than the connection's expected_account_id
  3  not found — the addressed chat, contact, file, or quoted message does
     not exist
  4  policy refusal — the project gate (store rows once the agent moved, else capabilities/settings.json) on any verb,
     or a write verb on a read-only connection (allow_write: false). Ask the user;
     never lift a gate yourself.
  5  server / network — the engine could not load, the connection failed, a
     round-trip timed out, an engine event handler failed, the phone did not
     answer a history request the read needed, a send failed or was not
     answered in time, or the store could not be reached or answered with an
     error
  6  input / validation — a malformed argument or date, an incoherent connection
     entry, an unsupported operation, or a store that is not configured or whose
     setting is invalid
  7  unlinked — no session for this connection, or the device was removed from
     the account. Re-link with `whatsapp pair`. Also what an empty read reports
     rather than answering with an absence it never established.
  8  busy — another invocation holds this session's lock, or the assistant
     service runs on the account (the message names it). Retry, or stop the
     service; never work around it, because two clients on one linked device
     desync the account.
  9  stalled — `service run` only: the listener made no progress for
     `defaults.stall_seconds`, or did not stop within 30s of SIGTERM, and
     left with every thread's stack in its log, for its supervisor to
     restart it.
"""

